## One code, not a password

The admissions portal doesn't ask you to invent a password. You identify yourself, and a **6-digit
one-time code (OTP)** is sent to you; entering it signs you in.

That means there's no password to forget between visits — but it also means you need access to
whatever you registered with each time you come back.

## Entering the code

Type the 6 digits and **Continue**. If the code doesn't arrive, **Request another OTP** sends a
fresh one.

Codes **expire**. If you're told yours has, that's a timing issue and not a sign anything is wrong
with your details — request another and use it promptly. Leaving the page open on an old code and
coming back to it later is the usual cause.

## Each institute has its own portal

The portal is per-institute: the address you use carries the school you're applying to. Applying to
two schools means two separate applications, each through its own link — there's no single account
spanning several institutes.

Keep the link the school sent you. Arriving at the wrong institute's portal is the usual reason an
application "disappears".

## If you see the unavailable screen

Admissions can be closed. If you reach a screen saying the portal isn't available, that institute
isn't accepting applications through it at the moment — it isn't a fault at your end, and
retrying or clearing your browser won't change it. Contact the school office to ask when
applications open.

